{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>They sacrificed.</b> To idolatry, which is an abhorrent thing." "<b>Not Elohim.</b> — who has no power and no strength, like ״ואת אילי הארץ לקח״. And Onkelos translated: that there is no need in them. But if there were any need in them, the jealousy would not be doubled as it is now. And a certain Roman said to Rabbi Shimon: 'Your El is a jealous one, as it is written ״אל קנא״ — does one become jealous except a mighty one against a mighty one? And it is written ״הם קנאוני בלא אל ואני אקניאם בלא עם״.' He said to him: 'Let me draw you a parable — to what may the thing be compared? To a man who married a woman exceedingly beautiful, and went and took another upon her, exceedingly ugly. The first one was angry with him and said to him: had you taken a worthy one, I would not have been particular — now that she is ugly, the vexation is doubled.' So said the Holy One, blessed be He: since you know that there is no substance in them, the jealousy is doubled.'" "<b>Elohim they did not know.</b> They did not recognize them, that there should be any benefit in them. And Rabbi Yosef Kara explained: because concerning the Holy One, blessed be He, it is written ״כי ידעתי את מכאוביו״, but they [the idols] did not know in their troubles and did not stand by them." "<b>New things that recently came.</b> They were not called Elohim until now." "<b>Your fathers never concerned themselves with them.</b> They did not visit them; פוקד we translate מסער."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:49.694584752Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}